// FIXED 2026-08-08. A fused DynCall re-ran a builtin on every kernel
// invocation even when no argument had fired, so any builtin with a
// per-invocation EFFECT repeated it. The node-walk ran it once.
//
//   interp: A 0 1 2 3 4 5              (one A)
//   jit:    A A A A A A 0 1 2 3 4 5    (one A per invocation)
//
// The block here is not a loop and not a recursion — it is evaluated
// once — so one A is the only defensible answer, and the node-walk was
// already correct. This was a kernel bug throughout.
//
// Two halves, both in the kernel:
//
// (1) DELIVERY. `DynCallSlot::dispatch` wrote every unmasked arg into
//     `event.variables`. A stale-masked slot now delivers ABSENCE, the
//     same as a taint-masked one: an argument that did not fire is not
//     an argument event, and the node-walk never manufactures one — a
//     builtin polls its arg nodes, and a node that did not produce
//     returns None while the builtin's own `CachedVals` slot rides.
//     (An earlier attempt delivered `TagValue::stale` instead. That
//     fixed the builtins which read the tag, but any builtin reading
//     PRESENCE still saw a phantom arrival.)
//
// (2) THE FIRE GATE. Delivering nothing is not enough: the call still
//     happens, so it advances the builtin's own state and burns the
//     inner Apply's one forced-init view. `graphix_dyncall` grew a
//     `dispatch` word — 0 means don't call at all, free the args and
//     take the pending path, so the site rides its cached result
//     through the taint cache that already exists. The emitter passes
//     `any_arg_fired | init_flag()`, computed from the RAW arg discs so
//     it stays decoupled from the delivery masks.
//
// And one half in the node-walk, which does NOT change this program but
// is the same rule: the print family gated on `is_some()` rather than
// the tag, so a constant re-delivered stale inside an evaluation frame
// re-printed. It now gates on `is_fired()`. Consequence, ruled: a print
// whose message is entirely constant, in a recursive body, prints once
// rather than per call. Interpolate anything that fires and it prints
// per update.
{
  let n = 0;
  select n { x if x < 5 => n <- (x ~ n) + 1, _ => never() };
  let r = { println("A"); n };
  r
}
